## what it is

A compatibility layer. The title's own x64 code runs on the host CPU. The console's system APIs
are reimplemented. The console's GPU shaders (GFX10 machine code) are recompiled to DXIL for
Direct3D 12. **No game files are in this repository.** Bring your own dump of a game you own.

```
title .exe (x64) ──► loader ──► console APIs (HLE) ──► Windows
                                       │
GFX10 shaders ──► decoder ──► IR ──► DXIL ──► D3D12 pipelines ──► your GPU
```

## progress

Measured on this fork, 25 Nov 2026. The numbers come from the tools in this repo
(`victory census`, `victory translate`, `victory warm`, `scripts/measure.ps1`).

| area | progress | detail |
|---|---|---|
| boot to main menu | `██████████` 100% | menus at a steady 30 fps |
| world entry | `██████████` 100% | the world loads and the story intro starts; the old hang on entry is fixed |
| imports resolved | `██████████` 99.8% | 995 across 18 images: 132 console APIs reimplemented, 789 passed to Windows, 72 served by the title's own DLLs, 2 missing |
| shaders translated | `██████████` 100% | 26,142 of 26,142 GPU programs to DXIL |
| compute pipelines ahead of time | `██████████` 98.6% | 2,697 of 2,734 built before the first run with `victory warm` |
| in-world frame rate | `██░░░░░░░░` | 5.6 to 6.6 fps in the first world scenes, about 3 fps in the heavy intro scene (about 7,000 draws a frame), from 0.5 to 1.9 before uploads were pooled, the shader cache kept across builds and copy-backs narrowed to the pages shaders wrote; `--console series-s` renders less. Binding on the title thread fell from about 515 to 218 ms a frame in that scene (upload and texture rings, a per-list memo of what is readable, the heap pass on a budget, fxceless's frame-rate work), so the frame waited on the card. Clamps of indirect draws now run once before each run of draws (from Belobug's fork): the card's waits on shader writes fell from about 13,400 to 710 a frame and its transitions from 19,600 to 414, the card is about 66% busy and the submitting thread is the limit again. Title memory sent to the card fell from about 450 to 140 MB a frame (writes tracked by 4 KiB piece, guarded blocks opened by piece), descriptor tables are built from a stamp and the heap mirror is compared a page at a time: the heavy scene runs at 230 to 285 ms a frame |
| picture | `█████░░░░░` | about 350 draws a frame are dropped in the heavy scene: of the two console pipeline creation methods that take raw GFX10 code and register state, compute (device slot 0x218) is written but off by default (`VICTORY_RAW_COMPUTE=1`) until it has run well in the game, and graphics (0x1c8) is not written yet |
| ray tracing | `███░░░░░░░` | the console's BVH builder translates (52 of 52 pipelines) and its ray instruction is lowered to inline DXIL; shadowed areas still come out dark |
| online | `░░░░░░░░░░` 0% | not a goal |

## what this fork adds

- **76 open upstream PRs merged**, the broken ones fixed first. Every merge and fix says why in its commit.
- **Both fxceless forks** folded in (the title-specific work and the fx PRs), kept up to date.
- **World entry no longer hangs.**
- **Pipelines build over 10x faster in game**: 2,436 in 420 s against 155 in 200 s before.
- **`victory warm`**: builds the title's compute pipelines into the pipeline library before you play,
  through the same code path a dispatch takes, so the first run loads them instead of stalling.

| | |
|---|---|
| Rust | 146,643 lines in 9 crates |
| tests | 962 |
| commits on top of upstream | over 600 |

## quick start

```powershell
cargo xtask install-hooks                 # asset-policy check on every commit
copy victory.example.toml victory.toml    # set your paths (outside the repo)
cargo build --profile diag -p victory --features diagnostics
target\diag\victory.exe warm <dump>       # optional, once: compute pipelines ahead of time (about 9 min)
target\diag\victory.exe run <dump>\<title>.exe --execute
```

Needs Windows 10/11 x64, an AVX2 CPU, a D3D12 GPU with shader model 6.6 and mesh shaders,
and Rust (version pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml`).

## commands

| command | what |
|---|---|
| `victory run <exe> --execute` | start the title |
| `victory warm <dump>` | build its compute pipelines into the library the runs load |
| `victory translate <dump>` | run every shader through the recompiler and list what it cannot handle (`--validate`, `--pipelines`) |
| `victory shader-dump <hash> --out <dir>` | a shader a run's log names, as its container and its code in SP3 syntax |
| `victory census <dump>` | which imports are reimplemented, passed through or missing |
| `victory inspect <file>` | one PE image: headers, sections, imports, TLS |
| `victory config` | the resolved configuration, checked |

## layout

| path | what |
|---|---|
| `apps/victory` | the command-line tool |
| `crates/` | loader, runtime services (HLE), GPU back end, shader recompiler, formats |
| `scripts/` | `measure.ps1` (one timed run with logs and an fps table), `list-prs.ps1` |
| `docs/` | architecture, asset policy, the full upstream guide ([docs/victory.md](docs/victory.md)) |
| `xtask/` | repository checks |

## contributing

Issues and pull requests are welcome. Keep commits small and say why in the message.
`cargo xtask policy` must pass: no game data, no screenshots, no binaries.

## legal

GPL-3.0-or-later, like upstream ([LICENSE](LICENSE)). Not affiliated with or endorsed by
Rockstar Games, Take-Two Interactive or Microsoft. Their names and marks belong to them.
`cargo xtask policy` refuses game data in any commit. See [docs/asset-policy.md](docs/asset-policy.md).

**Takedown requests:** if you are a game company that holds the rights to the title
and wants this repository gone, mail [hi@crt.fyi](mailto:hi@crt.fyi). It comes down, no lawyers needed.

## credits

[VIctory](https://github.com/Marvisak/VIctory) by Marvisak and contributors is the base of everything here.
[victory-gta6](https://github.com/fxceless/victory-gta6) by fxceless is the fork this one starts from.
[VIctory-Belobug-AMD](https://github.com/Belobug/VIctory-Belobug-AMD) by Belobug: indirect clamps hoisted before their draws, twice the dynamic descriptor slots, guards reopened in runs, screen-pass workers, large descriptor tables made once.
[XCL](https://github.com/xcl-dev/xcl) was a reference for how the same title behaves under another layer.

